1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:03,687
Today we meet two properties of whole numbers. Changing

2
00:00:03,687 --> 00:00:07,300
the order, and changing the grouping. Let's start with

3
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:08,531
a quick maths challenge!

4
00:00:09,223 --> 00:00:13,083
Here is a quick challenge: one hundred ninety six,

5
00:00:13,083 --> 00:00:16,006
plus fifty seven, plus four. Do we have to

6
00:00:16,006 --> 00:00:19,620
add from left to right? Watch carefully: the four

7
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:22,743
moves next to the one hundred ninety six. And

8
00:00:22,743 --> 00:00:25,963
one hundred ninety six plus four is two hundred,

9
00:00:25,963 --> 00:00:28,890
so we only need two hundred plus fifty seven.

10
00:00:28,890 --> 00:00:29,774
That looks easier!

11
00:00:30,413 --> 00:00:34,143
Let's begin with two small groups of counters: three

12
00:00:34,143 --> 00:00:38,573
and four. Together they make seven. Now the two

13
00:00:38,573 --> 00:00:41,985
groups change places; they still make seven. Three plus

14
00:00:41,985 --> 00:00:46,893
four equals four plus three. This is the commutative

15
00:00:46,893 --> 00:00:49,464
property: changing the order does not change the sum.

16
00:00:50,057 --> 00:00:54,057
The same idea works for multiplication. Here are twelve

17
00:00:54,057 --> 00:00:56,363
dots in four rows of three, so four times

18
00:00:56,363 --> 00:00:59,377
three is twelve. Now look at the very same

19
00:00:59,377 --> 00:01:02,210
twelve dots in three rows of four. Three times

20
00:01:02,210 --> 00:01:05,590
four is also twelve, so four times three equals

21
00:01:05,590 --> 00:01:06,508
three times four.

22
00:01:07,127 --> 00:01:09,327
We can write this rule with letters. For addition:

23
00:01:09,327 --> 00:01:11,721
a plus b equals b plus a. And for

24
00:01:11,721 --> 00:01:16,553
multiplication: a times b equals b times a. Changing

25
00:01:16,553 --> 00:01:19,998
the order never changes the answer.

26
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:27,070
Now something different. Three, four and five. If we

27
00:01:27,070 --> 00:01:29,106
add the first two first: three plus four is

28
00:01:29,106 --> 00:01:32,160
seven, and seven plus five is twelve. Look —

29
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:35,990
the numbers are still in the same order; only

30
00:01:35,990 --> 00:01:39,073
the grouping has changed. Now add the last two

31
00:01:39,073 --> 00:01:41,117
first: four plus five is nine, and three plus

32
00:01:41,117 --> 00:01:44,157
nine is twelve again. So three plus four, in

33
00:01:44,157 --> 00:01:47,470
a group, plus five, equals three plus, four plus

34
00:01:47,470 --> 00:01:50,801
five. That is the associative property.

35
00:01:51,427 --> 00:01:53,451
Multiplication obeys the same rule. Here are five rows

36
00:01:53,451 --> 00:01:56,940
of six capsules, so five times six is thirty.

37
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:01,561
Each capsule holds two dots, so thirty times two

38
00:02:01,561 --> 00:02:04,563
is sixty. But we could also start inside each

39
00:02:04,563 --> 00:02:07,267
row: six times two is twelve, and five rows

40
00:02:07,267 --> 00:02:12,218
of twelve make sixty. Different grouping, the same product.

41
00:02:12,723 --> 00:02:15,223
Let's keep these two ideas apart. On the left,

42
00:02:15,223 --> 00:02:18,723
the commutative property: the order of the numbers changes.

43
00:02:18,723 --> 00:02:22,837
On the right, the associative property: the order stays

44
00:02:22,837 --> 00:02:25,743
the same and only the grouping changes. Order, and

45
00:02:25,743 --> 00:02:28,714
grouping. Two different actions.

46
00:02:29,287 --> 00:02:32,401
Now back to our challenge: one hundred ninety six,

47
00:02:32,401 --> 00:02:35,889
plus fifty seven, plus four. First, let's group the

48
00:02:35,889 --> 00:02:38,775
last two terms. Inside the bracket we can swap

49
00:02:38,775 --> 00:02:43,335
them — that uses the commutative property. Then we

50
00:02:43,335 --> 00:02:47,987
regroup, and that uses the associative property. Now one

51
00:02:47,987 --> 00:02:51,387
hundred ninety six plus four is two hundred, and

52
00:02:51,387 --> 00:02:54,257
two hundred plus fifty seven is two hundred and

53
00:02:54,257 --> 00:02:55,278
fifty seven.

54
00:02:55,873 --> 00:02:58,349
Your turn! Find the sum of two hundred thirty

55
00:02:58,349 --> 00:03:01,553
eight, six hundred ninety five, and one hundred sixty

56
00:03:01,553 --> 00:03:04,733
two. Which two numbers can make a round hundred?

57
00:03:04,733 --> 00:03:06,364
Pause the video and try it.

58
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:14,433
Let's check. Two hundred thirty eight and one hundred

59
00:03:14,433 --> 00:03:18,180
sixty two make four hundred. So we put those

60
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:20,385
two together in one group, and add six hundred

61
00:03:20,385 --> 00:03:24,492
ninety five. Four hundred plus six hundred ninety five

62
00:03:24,492 --> 00:03:26,551
is one thousand and ninety five.

63
00:03:27,157 --> 00:03:30,817
Let's recap. Commutative means changing the order: a plus

64
00:03:30,817 --> 00:03:33,460
b equals b plus a, and a times b

65
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:37,209
equals b times a. Associative means changing the grouping:

66
00:03:37,209 --> 00:03:39,637
a plus b, in a group, plus c, equals

67
00:03:39,637 --> 00:03:42,545
a plus, b plus c; and a times b,

68
00:03:42,545 --> 00:03:45,508
in a group, times c, equals a times, b

69
00:03:45,508 --> 00:03:50,017
times c. Both work for addition and multiplication of

70
00:03:50,017 --> 00:03:54,408
whole numbers. Thank you for watching!
